            <ArticleTitle>Sleep Disordered Breathing Caused by Chronic Opioid Use: Diverse Manifestations and Their Management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>573-586</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1556-407X(17)30094-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jsmc.2017.07.007</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Opioid-induced sleep disordered breathing presents a therapeutic predicament with the increasing incidence of prescription opioid use for noncancer chronic pain in the United States. Central sleep apnea with a Biot or cluster breathing pattern is characteristic of polysomnography studies; however, long-term clinical outcomes and the impact of therapy remain unknown. Novel ampakine-based therapies are being investigated. Randomized controlled trials with therapies that target the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms of opioid-induced sleep disordered breathing are required.</AbstractText>
